    "patchSets": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "revision": "6a7c937267d52f31252a157154b90e53c22e9cd4",
            "parents": [
                "d9adc73a09cc17253ed8774dd72362ba5083d578"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/69/70269/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Marco Kawajiri",
                "email": "kawajirimarco@googlemail.com",
                "username": "kawmarco"
            },
            "createdOn": 1711559038,
            "author": {
                "name": "Marco Kawajiri",
                "email": "kawajirimarco@googlemail.com",
                "username": "kawmarco"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Why?  What are we trying to accomplish that isn't accomplished by simply increasing the default?  I'm pretty sure that 30 was picked arbitrarily as a \"we should have some limit\", but not a lot of thought was put into it.  IPMI already has a limit of 16, so we're past that by default with 30, so that isn't a concern.  If we increased this to, say, 128, could we avoid the option altogether?\n\nWhat limit would you expect to set here?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marco Kawajiri",
                        "email": "kawajirimarco@googlemail.com",
                        "username": "kawmarco"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't have context on the original limit so was trying to be conservative here. \n\nI'm testing internal builds with the limit set to 1000, one idea would be to reach this 1K limit and update the hardcoded limit, what do you think?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "If we set it to 1000 by default, and create 1000 users, what is the impact to the ROFS size?\n\nYou're right, 1000 is probably beyond where we could just do it without thinking about it, although I suspect that a lot of forks already have some mod like this, given they support LDAP.  If we can just change the default to 1000, that would be better.\n\nIf we're setting 1000, do we need to reorganize the dbus interfaces to make subfolders for the users?  An introspect of 1000 users sounds like a very long file.  How does the mapper operate in that case?  How does redfish AccountService operate?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "?  So these were added over SSH?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marco Kawajiri",
                        "email": "kawajirimarco@googlemail.com",
                        "username": "kawmarco"
                    },
                    "message": "Sorry, that was poorly worded, meant \"system already at the limit of system users\" (will fix the commit message).\n\nCurrent limits allow up to 15 users in the IPMI group and 30 - 15 = 15 non-IPMI users, 16 users was achieved by 1 IPMI user (root) + 15 non-IPMI users added through bmcweb"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> Sorry, that was poorly worded, meant \"system already at the limit of system users\" (will fix the commit message).\n\nRight.... but how did you get the system to that state?  That's the important part of your test.  \"I created 1000 users over redfish and observed that things work\" is a test, and is descriptive enough that someone can go reproduce it.  As-written, you just happen to get a system into a state it doesn't allow, so we should be looking for a bug.\n\n> \n> Current limits allow up to 15 users in the IPMI group and 30 - 15 = 15 non-IPMI users, 16 users was achieved by 1 IPMI user (root) + 15 non-IPMI users added through bmcweb"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "can we have another similar meson flag for max ipmi users?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> can we have another similar meson flag for max ipmi users?\n\nContributions welcome?  You shouldn't be asking a contributor to add an additional feature as part of a different commit.\n\nIPMI has limits due to the spec and the way passwords are required to be stored in plain-text.  This likely isn't as trivial as just making the same code changes as for base users."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "got it, my intent is to ask if contributor agrees, another meson flag for max ipmi users can be added in same commit."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marco Kawajiri",
                        "email": "kawajirimarco@googlemail.com",
                        "username": "kawmarco"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi Ravi, agree with Patrick here, probably better as a separate change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> can we have another similar meson flag for max ipmi users?\n\nWhy?  The max ipmi limit is 16 enforced by the specification.  I'm not sure why people would want to set fewer."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meson.options",
                    "line": 3,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This needs some reasonable upper bounds put on it.  I know for a fact that if I put 1 million here, it won't work.  I worry that even 1000 is too many to keep the DBus interfaces as they're working now performant."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meson.options",
                    "line": 3,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> I worry that even 1000 is too many to keep the DBus interfaces as they're working now performant.\n\nDbus doesn't get slower just because a process hosts a bunch of objects.  This seems to be a misconception that keeps getting stated (and was used against phosphor-logging).  I've created 30000 events in phosphor-logging with no BMC speed impact (other than it taking forever to write that many event files to the NOR flash)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meson.options",
                    "line": 3,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> > I worry that even 1000 is too many to keep the DBus interfaces as they're working now performant.\n> \n> Dbus doesn't get slower just because a process hosts a bunch of objects.\n>  This seems to be a misconception that keeps getting stated (and was used against phosphor-logging).  I've created 30000 events in phosphor-logging with no BMC speed impact (other than it taking forever to write that many event files to the NOR flash).\n\nCalls to Introspect, and GetManagedObjects definitely used to be slower.   \n The mapper responses and parsing on GetSubTreePaths and GetSubTree also were pretty slow for paths with a lot of children, as they're sending significantly larger payloads than if there were a single or no objects.  We still see this on systems with a lot of sensor instances, but maybe they're linear now instead of exponential? I will fully admit, I haven't tried an actual test recently.  Maybe I'm relying on old informat and this was performance data on much slower bmc (2500) than you guys have using a very old set of bindings/broker/dbus.  With that said, you're right, that wasn't something I should've brought up in this patch, and I was wrong to, as it's unimportant to the change I'm asking for.\n \nIn this patch, for this comment, my ask was that we should test (or at least hypothesize) what the upper limit is functionally ideally in both phosphor-user-manager and the mapper, and set the max parameter on the meson option.  If 1000 is what you guys need here and have tested the functional limits to, lets set that as the max, and call it a day."
                }
            ],
